---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="How a photo gets on screen — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="To open one photo, the app does one, two or three decodes, and the file format does not decide how many. This post follows the whole trip from a file on disk to pixels on your monitor."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-08">July 8, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>How a photo gets on screen</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          To open one photo, the app does one, two or three decodes, and the
          file format does not decide how many. This post follows the whole
          trip from a file on disk to pixels on your monitor.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          LightPhotos first does the fastest decode it can. If the result is
          as big as the window needs, it stops. If not, it runs a slower,
          correct decode. A third decode runs only when you zoom past the
          detail on screen. After that, zoom, pan and every slider are only
          math on the graphics card.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="decode-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-kind="raw" aria-pressed="true">Sony RAW</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-kind="jpeg" aria-pressed="false">JPEG</button>
          <label>Window needs <input type="range" min="800" max="3200" step="40" value="2560" aria-valuetext="2560 pixels"></label>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-zoom aria-pressed="false">Zoom past screen size</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 236" role="img" aria-label="Interactive: the size of the first decode compared with the size the window needs, and the decode passes that run as a result.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">THE CHECK AFTER PASS 1</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="34" data-result-label>Pass 1 result: 1616 px camera preview</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="0" y="44" width="303" height="16" rx="4" data-result-bar></rect>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="0" y1="66" x2="600" y2="66"></line>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="480" y1="38" x2="480" y2="72" stroke-width="2" data-window-line></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="480" y="90" text-anchor="middle" data-window-label>Window needs 2560 px</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="122">DECODES THAT RUN</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="132" width="180" height="30" rx="5" data-pass="1"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="90" y="151" text-anchor="middle" data-pass-label="1">Pass 1 &middot; 35 ms</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="188" y="132" width="240" height="30" rx="5" data-pass="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="308" y="151" text-anchor="middle" data-pass-label="2">Pass 2 &middot; 250 ms</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="436" y="132" width="203" height="30" rx="5" data-pass="3" opacity="0.35"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="538" y="151" text-anchor="middle" data-pass-label="3" opacity="0.35">Pass 3 &middot; full size, seconds</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="188" data-total>2 decodes</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="0" y1="204" x2="640" y2="204" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="226">Then: one upload to the GPU. A slider, zoom or pan sends only a few numbers.</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">The 1616 px preview is smaller than 2560 px. You see it after 35 ms, then the 250 ms decode swaps in.</p>
        <figcaption>
          The rule is the size of the result, not the file format. Make the
          window smaller than 1616 px and the RAW needs only one decode.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Three different jobs</h2>
      <p>
        A photo takes one of three paths through the app. I treat each path
        as its own problem.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Opening.</strong> The time from your click to the first
          image on screen is what counts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Browsing.</strong> The grid needs a small version of every
          visible photo, fast.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Exporting.</strong> The JPEG must be exactly right. It can
          take as long as it needs.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Opening a photo</h2>
      <p>
        A camera RAW file is the raw readout from the sensor. To turn it into
        a picture takes real work, called demosaicing. On my machine, that
        takes approximately 250 ms for a Sony ARW file at screen size.
      </p>
      <p>
        But your camera also saved a small JPEG preview inside the file. On my
        Sony, the preview is 1616 pixels on the long side, and it takes
        approximately 35 ms to extract. So the first pass gets the cheapest
        decode it can near the size I asked for. Then the app checks one
        thing.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        Is the result as big as the window actually needs?
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        If it is, the app stops after one decode. That happens with every
        JPEG, because the first pass decodes it at the requested size. It
        also happens with a RAW in a small window. If the loupe (the
        single-photo view) needs only 1400 pixels, the 1616-pixel preview is
        enough.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the result is too small, the app queues a second decode at the full
        target size and swaps it in. That is my usual case: my window asks
        for approximately 2560 pixels. A 250 ms wait became a 35 ms wait, but
        nothing actually got faster. The app shows something useful while the
        expensive work runs, and it skips that work when it is not needed.
      </p>
      <p>
        My first rule checked whether the file was a RAW. That would have
        grown into a list of special cases for each format. A check of the
        result size is one rule that covers every format, including formats I
        have not added yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third pass takes seconds and hundreds of megabytes of memory. So
        it runs only when you zoom in past the detail that the screen-size
        version has.
      </p>
      <p>
        When you use the arrow keys, the app loads the photos on each side
        ahead of time with only the cheap first pass. They do not compete with
        the photo you look at now.
      </p>

      <h2>After that, it all happens on the graphics card</h2>
      <p>
        The app sends a decoded photo to the graphics card (the GPU) once.
        Zooming and panning only change a few numbers that the app sends to
        the card each frame. Anything that happens 60 times a second must
        never redo the decode.
      </p>
      <p>
        The sliders work the same way. When you drag Exposure, only the new
        value goes to the GPU. A shader, a small program on the card, redraws
        the picture. The main processor never loops over the pixels.
      </p>

      <h2>Two ways of drawing</h2>
      <p>
        egui, a Rust interface library that redraws the whole interface every
        frame, draws the buttons, grid, filmstrip and sliders. A few hundred
        lines of hand-written GPU code draw the photo underneath a transparent
        panel.
      </p>
      <p>
        This split keeps the interface code loose and convenient, and the
        pixel-heavy part small enough to tune by hand. In a general-purpose
        toolkit, the photo is a widget, and a widget does not easily take a
        24-megapixel image with a custom shader.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the four builds meet</h2>
      <p>
        The macOS, Linux, Windows and browser builds differ in only one place:
        how they turn a file into pixels.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 280" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: four platform builds split only at the decode layer. Then they all use one shared edit pipeline, which feeds both the GPU tone shader and the exporter.">
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="70" y="20" text-anchor="middle">macOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="230" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Linux</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="320" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="390" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Windows</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="0" width="140" height="30" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="550" y="20" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 30 V52 M230 30 V52 M390 30 V52 M550 30 V52"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="73" text-anchor="middle">ImageIO</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="52" width="300" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="310" y="73" text-anchor="middle">Rust decoders</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="480" y="52" width="140" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="550" y="73" text-anchor="middle">same, in Web Workers</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M70 86 V100 H550 M550 86 V100 M310 86 V152"></path>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="0" y1="116" x2="640" y2="116" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="136">THE ONLY FORK. EVERYTHING BELOW IS SHARED</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M304 152 L316 152 L310 162 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="90" y="166" width="460" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="320" y="187" text-anchor="middle">One edit function, in linear light</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M200 200 V216 H440 M440 200 V216 M200 216 V230 M440 216 V230"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M194 230 L206 230 L200 240 Z M434 230 L446 230 L440 240 Z"></path>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="90" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="200" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Tone shader on the GPU</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="330" y="244" width="220" height="34" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="440" y="265" text-anchor="middle">Exported JPEG</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The platforms split only at the decode step. Everything after that is
          shared, so the platforms cannot drift apart.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The GPU tone shader is a direct translation of that one edit function.
        The function that bakes your edits into finished pixels has exactly two
        users: the exporter and the thumbnail you are looking at.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why an exported JPEG matches what you saw on screen. It is
        not a separate feature. It comes from one code path, and I prefer that
        to keeping two paths the same by hand.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <a href="/downloads.html">downloads page</a> has builds for all
        four platforms.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/learning-rust-while-the-agents-write-it.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Learning Rust while the agents write it</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">What ImageIO gives you free, and what it costs to leave</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    const fig = document.getElementById("decode-demo");
    if (fig) {
      const PREVIEW = 1616; // embedded preview on the author's Sony ARW
      const MAX = 3200; // right end of the size axis, in px
      const AXIS = 600; // axis width in SVG units
      const kindBtns = fig.querySelectorAll("[data-kind]");
      const zoomBtn = fig.querySelector("[data-zoom]");
      const range = fig.querySelector('input[type="range"]');
      const q = (sel) => fig.querySelector(sel);
      const readout = fig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      let kind = "raw";
      let zoom = false;

      const setPass = (n, on, last, label) => {
        const box = q(`[data-pass="${n}"]`);
        const text = q(`[data-pass-label="${n}"]`);
        box.setAttribute("class", on && last ? "lp-fig-box-accent" : "lp-fig-box");
        box.setAttribute("opacity", on ? "1" : "0.35");
        text.setAttribute("opacity", on ? "1" : "0.35");
        text.textContent = label;
      };

      const render = () => {
        const need = Number(range.value);
        range.setAttribute("aria-valuetext", `${need} pixels`);
        const result = kind === "raw" ? PREVIEW : need;
        const enough = result >= need;
        const x = (need / MAX) * AXIS;

        const bar = q("[data-result-bar]");
        bar.setAttribute("width", String((result / MAX) * AXIS));
        bar.setAttribute("class", enough ? "lp-fig-bar" : "lp-fig-bar-dim");
        q("[data-result-label]").textContent =
          kind === "raw"
            ? `Pass 1 result: ${PREVIEW} px camera preview`
            : `Pass 1 result: ${need} px, decoded at the requested size`;
        const line = q("[data-window-line]");
        line.setAttribute("x1", String(x));
        line.setAttribute("x2", String(x));
        const wl = q("[data-window-label]");
        wl.setAttribute("x", String(Math.min(Math.max(x, 80), 560)));
        wl.textContent = `Window needs ${need} px`;

        const two = !enough;
        const last = zoom ? 3 : two ? 2 : 1;
        setPass(1, true, last === 1, kind === "raw" ? "Pass 1 · 35 ms" : "Pass 1");
        setPass(2, two, last === 2, "Pass 2 · 250 ms");
        setPass(3, zoom, last === 3, "Pass 3 · full size, seconds");
        const count = 1 + (two ? 1 : 0) + (zoom ? 1 : 0);
        q("[data-total]").textContent = `${count} decode${count > 1 ? "s" : ""}`;

        let msg;
        if (kind === "jpeg") {
          msg = "A JPEG has no embedded preview. Pass 1 decodes it at the requested size, so it is always big enough.";
        } else if (enough) {
          msg = `The ${PREVIEW} px preview is big enough for ${need} px. The expensive decode never runs.`;
        } else {
          msg = `The ${PREVIEW} px preview is smaller than ${need} px. You see it after 35 ms, then the 250 ms decode swaps in.`;
        }
        if (zoom) msg += " You zoomed past the screen-size detail, so a full-size decode runs on demand.";
        readout.textContent = msg;
      };

      kindBtns.forEach((btn) =>
        btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
          kind = btn.dataset.kind;
          kindBtns.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === btn)));
          render();
        })
      );
      zoomBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
        zoom = !zoom;
        zoomBtn.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(zoom));
        render();
      });
      range.addEventListener("input", render);
      render();
    }
  </script>
</Base>
